1
00:00:00,382 --> 00:00:01,449
Here's something strange.

2
00:00:02,154 --> 00:00:06,108
Mendeleev arranged the elements in eighteen sixty-nine, by

3
00:00:06,148 --> 00:00:07,854
grouping elements with similar properties.

4
00:00:08,417 --> 00:00:11,616
The electron wasn't discovered until eighteen ninety-seven.

5
00:00:12,302 --> 00:00:14,156
Yet look at the modern table's shape.

6
00:00:14,721 --> 00:00:17,006
The left block is two columns wide, the

7
00:00:17,048 --> 00:00:19,172
middle block is ten, and the right block

8
00:00:19,193 --> 00:00:19,614
is six.

9
00:00:20,278 --> 00:00:22,368
Those are exactly the capacities of the s,

10
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:24,057
d and p subshells.

11
00:00:24,565 --> 00:00:25,033
Let's see how.

12
00:00:26,004 --> 00:00:27,878
Start with the rows, called periods.

13
00:00:28,401 --> 00:00:31,173
An element's period number is its highest occupied

14
00:00:31,314 --> 00:00:32,780
energy level, its outer shell.

15
00:00:33,383 --> 00:00:36,692
Across period three, from sodium to argon, every

16
00:00:36,773 --> 00:00:39,961
element is filling level three, first three s,

17
00:00:40,303 --> 00:00:41,064
then three p.

18
00:00:41,727 --> 00:00:43,675
Sodium ends in three s one.

19
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:46,829
Argon ends in three p six, with level

20
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:48,877
three's s and p both full.

21
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,805
Then potassium puts its next electron into level

22
00:00:51,946 --> 00:00:53,850
four, and a new period begins.

23
00:00:54,705 --> 00:00:55,637
Now the blocks.

24
00:00:56,181 --> 00:00:57,865
An element's block tells you the type of

25
00:00:57,905 --> 00:01:00,111
subshell its last electron went into.

26
00:01:00,732 --> 00:01:02,038
The two columns on the left are the

27
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:02,621
s block.

28
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:04,330
The six on the right are the p

29
00:01:04,371 --> 00:01:04,571
block.

30
00:01:05,014 --> 00:01:06,398
The ten in the middle are the d

31
00:01:06,439 --> 00:01:09,148
block, and the two separate rows underneath are

32
00:01:09,168 --> 00:01:11,075
the f block, fourteen wide.

33
00:01:11,638 --> 00:01:13,046
That's why the table has this shape.

34
00:01:13,529 --> 00:01:15,277
Each block is as wide as the number

35
00:01:15,298 --> 00:01:17,066
of electrons its subshell can hold.

36
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:19,887
The columns are groups, and this is where

37
00:01:19,907 --> 00:01:21,191
the pattern really pays off.

38
00:01:21,994 --> 00:01:24,321
Valence electrons are the electrons in the outer

39
00:01:24,361 --> 00:01:24,602
shell.

40
00:01:25,303 --> 00:01:27,827
Down group one, lithium ends in two s

41
00:01:27,988 --> 00:01:30,952
one, sodium in three s one, and potassium

42
00:01:31,034 --> 00:01:31,894
in four s one.

43
00:01:32,617 --> 00:01:35,612
Different levels, but always one valence electron, in

44
00:01:35,633 --> 00:01:36,476
an s subshell.

45
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:40,025
Down group seventeen, every element ends in s

46
00:01:40,106 --> 00:01:42,931
two, p five, so seven valence electrons.

47
00:01:43,593 --> 00:01:45,217
For groups one and two, the number of

48
00:01:45,238 --> 00:01:47,202
valence electrons equals the group number.

49
00:01:47,726 --> 00:01:49,961
For groups thirteen to eighteen, subtract ten.

50
00:01:50,726 --> 00:01:51,712
Helium has two.

51
00:01:52,389 --> 00:01:52,972
Quick prediction.

52
00:01:53,597 --> 00:01:56,200
An atom has the configuration one s two,

53
00:01:56,662 --> 00:01:59,525
two s two, two p six, three s

54
00:01:59,566 --> 00:02:02,169
two, three p six, four s two.

55
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:04,988
Which period, and which group is it in?

56
00:02:08,709 --> 00:02:11,387
Period four, because its highest level is four.

57
00:02:12,091 --> 00:02:14,638
Group two, because it has two valence electrons,

58
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:16,002
in an s subshell.

59
00:02:16,446 --> 00:02:17,211
It's calcium.

60
00:02:17,899 --> 00:02:18,769
Here's a classic trap.

61
00:02:19,337 --> 00:02:22,053
An element with six valence electrons isn't in

62
00:02:22,094 --> 00:02:22,637
group six.

63
00:02:23,323 --> 00:02:24,775
Group six is in the d block.

64
00:02:25,482 --> 00:02:28,424
Six valence electrons, s two p four, puts

65
00:02:28,465 --> 00:02:29,446
it in group sixteen.

66
00:02:30,096 --> 00:02:32,069
Here's why groups matter so much.

67
00:02:32,714 --> 00:02:35,340
A chemical property describes how a substance reacts

68
00:02:35,401 --> 00:02:36,603
to form new substances.

69
00:02:37,085 --> 00:02:39,408
A physical property can be observed without making

70
00:02:39,469 --> 00:02:42,774
a new substance, like state, colour, or conductivity.

71
00:02:43,617 --> 00:02:46,186
Elements in a group share their valence arrangement,

72
00:02:46,549 --> 00:02:48,195
so they react in similar ways.

73
00:02:48,898 --> 00:02:51,362
Every group one metal reacts with water, making

74
00:02:51,443 --> 00:02:53,546
hydrogen gas and an alkaline solution.

75
00:02:54,248 --> 00:02:57,398
The halogens in group seventeen are all reactive

76
00:02:57,419 --> 00:02:59,746
non-metals, made of pairs of atoms.

77
00:03:00,429 --> 00:03:02,434
And the noble gases, with a full outer

78
00:03:02,475 --> 00:03:03,958
shell, hardly react at all.

79
00:03:04,949 --> 00:03:07,455
Position also tells us about physical properties.

80
00:03:08,058 --> 00:03:09,882
Metals sit on the left and in the

81
00:03:09,923 --> 00:03:12,208
middle, with just a few valence electrons.

82
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:15,599
They're shiny, they conduct heat and electricity, and

83
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:17,867
they're malleable, which means they can be hammered

84
00:03:17,908 --> 00:03:18,349
into shape.

85
00:03:19,234 --> 00:03:20,972
Non-metals sit at the top right.

86
00:03:21,676 --> 00:03:24,244
As solids they're dull and brittle, they don't

87
00:03:24,265 --> 00:03:26,533
conduct electricity, and many are gases.

88
00:03:27,397 --> 00:03:29,989
Along the staircase between them are the metalloids,

89
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:30,853
like silicon.

90
00:03:31,477 --> 00:03:33,901
Silicon is shiny like a metal, but brittle

91
00:03:34,002 --> 00:03:36,847
like a non-metal, and it conducts electricity only

92
00:03:36,868 --> 00:03:37,308
a little.

93
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:39,458
That's why it's used in computer chips.

94
00:03:40,448 --> 00:03:43,316
Let's walk across period three at room temperature.

95
00:03:44,059 --> 00:03:47,129
Sodium, magnesium and aluminium are solid metals.

96
00:03:47,874 --> 00:03:49,668
Silicon is a solid metalloid.

97
00:03:50,274 --> 00:03:52,731
Phosphorus and sulfur are solid non-metals.

98
00:03:53,496 --> 00:03:56,007
Then chlorine is a pale green gas, and

99
00:03:56,229 --> 00:03:57,634
argon is a colourless gas.

100
00:03:58,499 --> 00:04:01,046
So across a period, elements change from metals

101
00:04:01,227 --> 00:04:02,049
to non-metals.

102
00:04:02,632 --> 00:04:05,276
And across the whole table, only eleven elements

103
00:04:05,377 --> 00:04:06,818
are gases at room temperature,

104
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:10,245
and only two are liquids, bromine and mercury.

105
00:04:10,751 --> 00:04:11,882
All the rest are solids.

106
00:04:12,576 --> 00:04:13,401
Here's a surprise.

107
00:04:14,046 --> 00:04:16,801
The halogens share their chemistry, but not their

108
00:04:16,863 --> 00:04:17,204
state.

109
00:04:17,948 --> 00:04:19,674
Fluorine and chlorine are gases,

110
00:04:20,177 --> 00:04:22,566
bromine is a liquid, and iodine is a

111
00:04:22,667 --> 00:04:23,028
solid.

112
00:04:23,651 --> 00:04:25,757
Same valence arrangement, different states.

113
00:04:26,581 --> 00:04:30,049
Explaining that needs the forces between molecules, which

114
00:04:30,230 --> 00:04:31,212
come later in this module.

115
00:04:32,015 --> 00:04:34,360
So a group shares its chemistry, but not

116
00:04:34,541 --> 00:04:35,603
every physical property.

117
00:04:36,457 --> 00:04:37,080
So here's the map.

118
00:04:37,726 --> 00:04:38,963
Row, outer shell.

119
00:04:39,548 --> 00:04:41,034
Column, outer electrons.

120
00:04:41,698 --> 00:04:43,212
Block, last subshell.

121
00:04:43,980 --> 00:04:46,008
Read those from a configuration, and you can

122
00:04:46,049 --> 00:04:48,941
place any element, and predict how it behaves.

123
00:04:51,704 --> 00:04:52,188
Your turn.

124
00:04:52,892 --> 00:04:55,723
Selenium has atomic number thirty-four.

125
00:04:56,445 --> 00:04:59,649
Write its electron configuration, then state its period,

126
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:01,492
group and block.

127
00:05:02,213 --> 00:05:05,222
Name one element with similar chemical properties, and

128
00:05:05,383 --> 00:05:06,426
explain why.

129
00:05:07,090 --> 00:05:08,257
Pause here to answer.

130
00:05:19,028 --> 00:05:22,676
Selenium is argon, three d ten, four s

131
00:05:22,737 --> 00:05:24,080
two, four p four.

132
00:05:24,923 --> 00:05:27,109
Its highest level is four, so it's in

133
00:05:27,210 --> 00:05:27,891
period four.

134
00:05:28,655 --> 00:05:30,884
Its outer shell holds four s two, four

135
00:05:30,925 --> 00:05:33,395
p four, which is six valence electrons.

136
00:05:34,078 --> 00:05:36,025
The full three d isn't in the outer

137
00:05:36,066 --> 00:05:36,326
shell.

138
00:05:37,192 --> 00:05:39,146
So it's group sixteen, in the p block.

139
00:05:39,850 --> 00:05:42,931
Sulfur, or oxygen, has similar chemistry, because it

140
00:05:42,992 --> 00:05:45,573
has the same outer arrangement, s two p

141
00:05:45,614 --> 00:05:45,874
four.

142
00:05:46,596 --> 00:05:48,443
Putting it in group six is the classic

143
00:05:48,464 --> 00:05:48,684
slip.

144
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:50,771
Last one.

145
00:05:51,598 --> 00:05:53,636
Three elements are described by their properties.

146
00:05:54,259 --> 00:05:57,341
A is shiny, conducts electricity well, can be

147
00:05:57,422 --> 00:05:59,502
hammered into shape, and melts at six hundred

148
00:05:59,523 --> 00:06:00,303
and sixty degrees.

149
00:06:01,146 --> 00:06:04,399
B is yellow and brittle, doesn't conduct, and

150
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,045
melts at a hundred and fifteen degrees.

151
00:06:06,849 --> 00:06:10,215
C is shiny grey and brittle, conducts slightly,

152
00:06:10,557 --> 00:06:13,201
and melts at fourteen hundred and fourteen degrees.

153
00:06:13,904 --> 00:06:16,110
Classify each, and say where each type is

154
00:06:16,191 --> 00:06:17,514
found in the periodic table.

155
00:06:18,158 --> 00:06:18,903
Pause here to answer.

156
00:06:29,620 --> 00:06:30,370
A is a metal.

157
00:06:30,977 --> 00:06:33,968
It's shiny, conducts well and is malleable, and

158
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:35,857
metals sit on the left and in the

159
00:06:35,878 --> 00:06:36,058
middle.

160
00:06:36,826 --> 00:06:37,895
B is a non-metal.

161
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:41,375
It's brittle and doesn't conduct, and non-metals sit

162
00:06:41,457 --> 00:06:42,240
at the top right.

163
00:06:43,143 --> 00:06:46,425
C is a metalloid, shiny but brittle, and

164
00:06:46,546 --> 00:06:48,946
only slightly conducting, so it sits on the

165
00:06:48,987 --> 00:06:49,467
staircase.

166
00:06:50,249 --> 00:06:52,660
Calling C a metal, just because it's shiny,

167
00:06:52,963 --> 00:06:54,028
is the classic slip.

168
00:06:54,532 --> 00:06:57,270
Brittleness and weak conduction say metalloid.

169
00:06:59,203 --> 00:07:01,860
So electron arrangements organise the whole table.

170
00:07:02,402 --> 00:07:04,724
The period gives the outer shell, the group

171
00:07:04,824 --> 00:07:07,407
gives the valence electrons, and the block gives

172
00:07:07,447 --> 00:07:08,548
the last subshell.

173
00:07:09,189 --> 00:07:10,556
Now, think about size.

174
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:13,610
Sodium and chlorine both have three shells.

175
00:07:14,194 --> 00:07:15,167
Are they the same size?

176
00:07:15,795 --> 00:07:18,283
And potassium has one more shell than sodium.

177
00:07:19,006 --> 00:07:21,854
Next, why does atomic radius change across a

178
00:07:21,915 --> 00:07:23,559
period and down a group?

179
00:07:26,361 --> 00:07:27,184
Here are the key notes.

180
00:07:27,707 --> 00:07:29,255
Pause the video if you need more time

181
00:07:29,295 --> 00:07:29,717
to copy them.
